First: Determine the nature of the source of the fear being experienced. Be as specific as you can about what is generating the fear within you. Ask yourself: 

Is it a potentially illogical, but not entirely implausible, thought or belief you hold? 
Is it a current or future external person or relationship or situation which you find to be threatening in some way?  
Is it the expectation of a future hardship or difficulty due to a personal defect, shortcoming, or unjustifiable reliance on the actions of someone else? 

If you cannot confidently say yes to any of these, allowing for a broad and general reading of the phrasing or semantics, stop now. Your fear is likely to be a more reliable interpretation of your experience than any potentiality which you are capable of discerning cognitively. It is vital that you recognize this outcome as a success in the adaptations of Life across inconceivable lengths of time - rather than as a failure of your (relatively) newfound capacity for complex, highly-accurate conscious cognition (your "IQ" version of intelligence, basically). 

If you can confidently say yes to at least one of these questions, determine the most specific phrasing you can use to understand why the "what" of your fear is within some parameter of the questions. Stated another way; make the case for why you said yes, and be as concise as possible. 

If you cannot (barring a great deal of effort)  condense this case-statement to a single sentence that maintains a reasonable degree of coherence, stop now.  Your situation is likely too complex to ignore your feelings; or, you have not yet developed the discipline and awareness to confidently dismiss something as primary as fear.  #The causailty* is {(pb'dn)|possibly both, doubtfully neither| truth_v3.1=useful;optional}#

If you can state the why of the what -  confidently and concisely and contextually-appropriate - congratulations! 
